  grammar: [
    { q: '(A) Although the student body (B) continue to get smaller, the superintendent (C) insists that the high school (D) is thriving. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Collective noun "student body" is singular → requires "continues".' },
    { q: '(A) Everyone (B) who plans (C) to attend the senior retreat (D) need to pack appropriate camping supplies. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 3, exp: 'Indefinite pronoun "Everyone" is singular → requires "needs".' },
    { q: '(A) The strangely distorted, colorful pictures by Picasso (B) has received much attention (C) not only from the critics, (D) but also from the general public. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'The subject is plural "pictures" → requires "have received".' },
    { q: '(A) The Grapes of Wrath (B) are one of my favorite books, (C) but I preferred the original movie (D) when I saw it. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'A book title is a singular entity → requires "is".' },
    { q: '(A) A number of criminals (B) remains at large and (C) continue to transgress the law (D) with wanton impunity. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: '"A number of..." takes a plural verb → requires "remain".' },
    { q: '(A) Neither the manager nor (B) his assistants (C) was present at the meeting (D) yesterday. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 2, exp: 'With "Neither...nor", the verb agrees with the nearest subject ("assistants", plural) → "were".' },
    { q: '(A) Each of the applicants (B) are required to submit (C) their updated resume (D) before Friday. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: '"Each" is singular → requires "is required".' },
    { q: '(A) Physics (B) are a difficult subject (C) for many students (D) entering college. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Academic disciplines ending in -s are singular → requires "is".' },
    { q: '(A) Ten miles (B) are a long distance (C) to walk (D) on a hot afternoon. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Measurements of distance treated as a single unit are singular → requires "is".' },
    { q: '(A) Measles (B) are an infectious disease (C) that typically affects (D) young children. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Disease names like measles, mumps, and rickets are singular → requires "is".' },
    { q: '(A) The Philippines (B) consist of more than (C) seven thousand islands (D) and islets. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'The name of a country takes a singular verb → requires "consists".' },
    { q: '(A) Bread and butter (B) are my favorite (C) breakfast meal (D) every morning. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'When two items express a single compound food concept, it is singular → requires "is".' },
    { q: '(A) Most of the money (B) have been spent (C) on medical supplies (D) and equipment. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: '"Money" is an uncountable mass noun → requires "has been spent".' },
    { q: '(A) Hard work and perseverance (B) is the key (C) to achieving success (D) in life. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Two distinct virtues joined by "and" take a plural verb → requires "are".' },
    { q: '(A) The committee (B) are divided in their opinions (C) regarding the new office policy. (D) No error. (E)', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 4, exp: 'No error — when members of a collective noun act as individuals with opposing views, the plural verb is correct.' },
    { q: '(A) The police (B) is investigating (C) the robbery that occurred (D) downtown. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: '"Police" is always treated as a plural noun → requires "are investigating".' },
    { q: '(A) She is one of those women (B) who works hard (C) to achieve her goals (D) in life. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: '"Who" modifies the plural noun "women" → requires plural verb "work hard".' },
    { q: '(A) Every man, woman, and child (B) were evacuated (C) safely before the storm (D) made landfall. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Subjects preceded by "Every" are strictly singular → requires "was evacuated".' },
    { q: '(A) The quality (B) of these imported mangoes (C) are far superior (D) to local ones. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 2, exp: 'The subject is "quality" (singular), not "mangoes" → requires "is far superior".' },
    { q: '(A) My uncle, along with his friends, (B) are planning to open (C) a new restaurant (D) next month. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Parenthetical phrases like "along with" do not alter subject number → requires "is planning".' },
    { q: '(A) Supposing if it rains (B) tomorrow, where will we hold (C) the outdoor graduation (D) ceremony? (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: 'Redundancy — use either "Supposing" or "If", never both together.' },
    { q: '(A) Despite of his failing health, (B) the old man continued (C) to write his memoirs (D) every single day. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: '"Despite" never takes the preposition "of". Say "Despite his failing health" or "In spite of".' },
    { q: '(A) He is senior (B) than me (C) by two years in the government (D) service. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: 'Latin comparatives (senior, junior, superior, preferable) take "to", not "than" → "senior to me".' },
    { q: '(A) Returning back to his hometown, (B) Mark realized how much (C) the community had changed (D) over the years. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: 'Redundancy — "Returning" already includes the meaning of "back". Omit "back".' },
    { q: '(A) Between you and I, (B) the project proposed (C) is unlikely to succeed (D) this quarter. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: 'The preposition "between" requires object pronouns → "Between you and me".' },
    { q: '(A) The design (B) is similar (C) with the old cathedral (D) in downtown Manila. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 2, exp: 'The correct idiom is "similar to", not "similar with".' },
    { q: '(A) She is looking forward (B) to see her family (C) after working abroad (D) for five years. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 1, exp: '"Look forward to" is followed by a gerund (-ing) → "to seeing".' },
    { q: '(A) If I was the director, (B) I would implement stricter policies (C) to reduce corruption (D) in the agency. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: 'Contrary-to-fact subjunctive requires "were" → "If I were the director".' },
    { q: '(A) Unless you do not submit (B) your requirements on time, (C) your application will be (D) disqualified. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: '"Unless" already contains a negative meaning → omit "do not".' },
    { q: '(A) She is the most smartest student (B) in our graduating class (C) this academic (D) year. (E) No error.', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E'], ans: 0, exp: 'Double comparison error — use "smartest", never "most smartest".' },
  ],

